; ..\BSW364\Os\Implementation\Os_Trap.c	   155  /*! \brief          Returns whether caller of an API has to trap for read access.
; ..\BSW364\Os\Implementation\Os_Trap.c	   156   *  \details        Performing a trap is typically very time consuming. For this reason the OS tries to prevent it.
; ..\BSW364\Os\Implementation\Os_Trap.c	   157   *                  If the current thread has read privileges, the OS does not have to trap. On some platforms this
; ..\BSW364\Os\Implementation\Os_Trap.c	   158   *                  optimization is not possible, because the hardware does not allow to efficiently check whether the
; ..\BSW364\Os\Implementation\Os_Trap.c	   159   *                  current thread has read privileges. In this case, UserMode is assumed as soon as MemoryProtection
; ..\BSW364\Os\Implementation\Os_Trap.c	   160   *                  is enabled.
; ..\BSW364\Os\Implementation\Os_Trap.c	   161   *
; ..\BSW364\Os\Implementation\Os_Trap.c	   162   *  \retval         !0  Trap is required, caller thread does not have read privileges.
; ..\BSW364\Os\Implementation\Os_Trap.c	   163   *  \retval         0   Trap is not required, caller thread has read privileges.
; ..\BSW364\Os\Implementation\Os_Trap.c	   164   *
; ..\BSW364\Os\Implementation\Os_Trap.c	   165   *  \context        OS_INTERNAL
; ..\BSW364\Os\Implementation\Os_Trap.c	   166   *
; ..\BSW364\Os\Implementation\Os_Trap.c	   167   *  \reentrant      TRUE
; ..\BSW364\Os\Implementation\Os_Trap.c	   168   *  \synchronous    TRUE
